<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohéli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mwali, Moheli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohéli, or Mwali, is the smallest of the four main islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a> and Anjouan. Long claimed by the sultans of Anjouan, it was seized around 1830 by the Malagasy prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanetaka">Ramanetaka</a>, whose family ruled it as sultans until it became a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1886. It is now an autonomous island of the Union of the Comoros, known for its marine park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moheli.jpg" alt="Mohéli" width="262" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moh%C3%A9li_in_Comoros.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
